\chapter{Optimization profiles and zero-cost comparisons}
\label{ch:87}
\index{Optimization profiles and zero-cost comparisons}

\OnxCodeTerm{default}, \OnxCodeTerm{debug}, and \OnxCodeTerm{release} select enabled HIR rules, decision lowering and applicable MIR passes. An explicit rule selection narrows a question such as whether one neutral rewrite occurred. Paired benchmarks compare authored and wrapped examples under equivalent profiles. Code or artifact identity in a tested pair is evidence for that pair and configuration.

\section{Compare observable costs separately}
Verify both programs have the same results and traps. Record whether checked C, normalized MIR, assembly or linked native code agree, and report the normalization used. Measure compilation and execution separately. Include repeated runs and a comparable baseline. A macro or source abstraction might disappear in runtime code and still add build work or provenance metadata. Borrowing, allocation and foreign calls need their own comparisons. A benchmark on scalar wrappers does not establish their behavior on those other profiles.

\section{Derive the checksum before comparing the code}
In the zero-cost fixture let $r$ be the iteration plus a fixed seed,
reduced modulo seven. The explicit program compares the predicate
$r<3$ with the parity test for the iteration. If the two Booleans
agree it adds $r$ to a running total; otherwise it subtracts one. The
wrapped program surrounds the same decisions with neutral arithmetic
and Boolean operators. Both contain the predicate call. Removing that
call from just one baseline would answer a different performance question.

Within fourteen iterations each residue appears twice, once on each
parity: advancing by seven preserves the residue and flips parity.
For a particular $r$ the predicate stays fixed, so exactly one of its
two iterations contributes $r$ and the other contributes negative one.
Adding $r-1$ over residues zero through six gives fourteen. A checksum
over complete cycles alone would also pass if the two branch decisions
were exchanged! The measured million-iteration case has a partial cycle.
At seed 1729 the expected total is 999998; reversing the decision gives
999999. The tail makes a seemingly innocuous predicate regression visible.

Only after both runs meet this oracle compare the artifacts. For MIR
the gate normalizes source coordinates but retains block arguments,
instruction order and constants; for the linked ELF it strips symbols
and removes the build-ID note. If the normalized artifacts match while
timings differ, report both observations. Timing includes launch costs
and compilation includes work needed to simplify the wrappers. Neither
measurement contradicts an equality of the compared bytes.

